{
  "abstract": "The central contribution is a Lean 4 / Mathlib proof that Mathlib's `RiemannHypothesis` implies two classical statements: for every delta > 0, |psi(x) - x| <= C x^(1/2 + delta) for all large x (`Submission.chebyshev_psi_error_of_RH`, psi being Mathlib's `Chebyshev.psi`), and for every epsilon > 0 and all large x a prime in (x, x + x^(1/2 + epsilon)] (`Submission.exists_prime_in_short_interval_of_RH`). Both are known under RH: the first is the theorem of section 7 of H. von Koch (1901), who also proves the sharper sqrt(x) log^2 x, and the second follows from it; this work does not claim them as new mathematics. Their proof is a contour shift of the smoothed Chebyshev function of PrimeNumberTheoremAnd (whose files are ported to an earlier Mathlib with mechanical changes, `PNTPort/`) to the line Re s = 1/2 + delta, with two new estimates: zeta'/zeta << log|t| to the right of 1/2 under RH (Borel-Caratheodory on a holomorphic logarithm of zeta), and an interpolated bound on the Mellin transform of the smoothing that keeps the exponent 1/2 (the bound used in PrimeNumberTheoremAnd alone would give 3/4). PrimeNumberTheoremAnd records RH-conditional prime-in-interval statements of this kind as targets with `sorry`. Zero sorries; the advertised theorems depend only on the three standard Lean axioms.",
